## INTRODUCTION
Ethylene $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{4})}$ is the largest feedstock in petrochemical industries. The worldwide production of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (over 150 million tons in 2016) exceeds any other organic compound primarily because of its use in the manufacture of polyethylene.1,2 Steam cracking is the principal industrial method for producing light alkenes, including $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ .3-5 However, this method alone does not yield high-purity $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ $(\geq99.95\%$ , polymer-grade), in which ethane $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{6})}$ is usually one of the major concomitants. Further purification, such as cryogenic distillation, is required to produce commercial polymer-grade $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ ,6 but this process is extremely energyintensive as it necessitates the use of large distillation columns with 120 to 180 trays for high reflux ratios due to their similar molecular size and volatility.7,8
Among the potential alternatives to replace cryogenic distillation for hydrocarbon separation, selective adsorption is one of the most desired technologies because of low energy consumption.9,10 A variety of microporous adsorbents have been examined for the separation of light hydrocarbons, and metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are one of the most promising candidates due to their large accessible surface areas, tunable pore sizes and chemistries.11-17 Particularly, MOFs with open metal sites (OMSs) have been extensively studied because they preferentially interact with unsaturated hydrocarbons through metal $\pi$ -complexation, and thus separate them from their saturated counterparts.18-20 For example, the material $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ exhibited excellent performance in hydrocarbon separation with a superior selectivity for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ over $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ thanks to the high-density OMSs engaging in sideon coordination of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ . The adsorption enthalpies for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ were calculated to be – 45 and – $-\ 25\ \mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ , respectively, and the coordinatively unsaturated $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ ions were determined as the strongest adsorption sites in the material by neutron powder diffraction analysis.21 Nevertheless, the OMSs in MOFs could be easily poisoned in the presence of water vapor during the hydrocarbon separation owing to the much higher binding energy of water molecule to metal cations. Interestingly, prepared via the addition of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ in $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ , the material $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})$ (dobdc) was shown an inversion of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity with a surprisingly large adsorption enthalpy of $-\ 67\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ , leading to an unprecedented $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity of 4.4 for a 1:1 mixture at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar. Neutron powder diffraction data indicated that the introduced Fe-peroxo sites engage in close contacts with one of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ groups of the adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ molecules. However, its performance under humid conditions was not discussed, which we suspect would be discouraging as both $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})$ (dobdc) are air-sensitive and needed to be handled in a dry box under an $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere.22
Aside from OMSs, molecular sieving appears to be another straightforward design of MOF materials for hydrocarbon separation. Such size-exclusion mechanism can be applied to the separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture as the former being relatively small in molecular size. For example, the ultramicroporous MOF $\mathrm{[Ca(C_{4}O_{4})(H_{2}O)]}$ , possessing rigid onedimensional channels with an aperture size similar to ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ but smaller than $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ molecule, was shown to function as a molecular sieve, preventing the transport of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ molecules inside the channels.23 Nevertheless, molecular sieving of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ is less than ideal in terms of energy efficiency because further desorption is necessary in order to get $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ . In addition, the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ residing in the mobile phase (in between adsorbent particles) may contaminate the final $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ product during the desorption stage.24 By this token, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ -selective adsorbents would be more energy-efficient by directly producing high-purity $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ .
Molecular docking, a process of placing preferred guest molecule in a receptor’s binding site in biology, can also be applied in designing MOF materials for hydrocarbon separation, which would preferentially adsorb guest molecules of particular size and shape, not necessarily the smaller ones. Thus, the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ adsorption selectivity in favor of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ could be reversed by precisely tuning the pore structure to better complement ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ instead of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ molecule. For example, the material MAF-49 exhibited a large ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ adsorption enthalpy of – $60\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ at low coverage. Singlecrystal $X$ -ray diffraction and computational simulation studies showed that the primary adsorption site could accommodate either $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ or $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ , and the exceptional $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ selectivity derives from the proper positioning of multiple electronegative functional groups on the ultra-microporous pore surface, which results in more $\mathrm{C-H\cdotsN}$ hydrogen bonds with ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ than $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ . In other words, the larger ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ molecules engage in stronger host-guest interactions with MAF-49.25
Despite the excellent ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) selectivity $(\sim9)$ in favor of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ for an equimolar $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture at $316\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar, MAF-49 exhibits a relatively low capacity for ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ $\mathrm{(1.7~mmol/g)}$ under the same conditions. The construction of single-molecular pocket for accommodating one molecule at a time could compromise the surface area and potentially limit the overall uptake. Adequate adsorption capacity is nonetheless desired for adsorbents to be economically viable; therefore, an ideal adsorbent should exhibit both high adsorption capacity and selectivity. Unfortunately, these two key parameters are often trade-off to each other and appear to be difficult to improve simultaneously. To balance between selectivity (host-guest interaction) and capacity (surface area), a possible solution would be to design MOF materials featuring large cavities interconnected through tailored apertures; the apertures function as screening sites for desired gas molecules to go through, while the internal cavities provide surface for large adsorption.
To date, not many $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ -selective MOFs have been identified,22,23,26-34,35 and only a handful of them show favorable adsorption capacity for ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ $\mathrm{(>3~mmol/g}$ at 1 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ ),22,23,33,34,35 let alone MOFs that are capable of separating ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ gas mixtures under humid conditions. Herein, we report the successful construction of a multicomponent microporous MOF (JNU-2, JNU $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ Jinan University) underlying a rare xae topology. It possesses three cage-like cavities interconnected by apertures. In particular, the one connecting two smaller cavities bears a channellimiting diameter36 of ca. $3.7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , which is in the domain of kinetic diameter of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ molecules. Molecular modeling studies suggest the rich electronegativity on the aperture may facilitate a stronger interaction with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ than $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and function as a screening site for preferentially taking in ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ during the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation. Indeed, breakthrough experiments reveal that JNU-2 is capable of effectively separating ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ gas mixtures of different ratios and demonstrating excellent separation capacity even under humid conditions. Together with its acid/base tolerance and room-temperature regeneration, JNU2 is potentially an ideal adsorbent for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ purification under real industrial conditions.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials and Physical Measurements.
All reagents and materials were obtained commercially and used as received without further purification. $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , adenine and 4-pyrazolecarboxylic acid were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent $\mathrm{Co}$ . Ltd. Ultrahigh-purity-grade $(>99.999\%$ ) $\Nu_{2}$ , Ar, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ gases were used for adsorption-related measurements. Thermogravimetric analyses were performed under a ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere with a temperature increasing rate of $10\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C\ m i n^{\cdot1}}$ on a TA-Q50 system. Powder x-ray diffraction (PXRD) data were collected on a Rigaku Ultima IV diffractometer $(40\mathrm{~kV}$ , $40~\mathrm{mA}$ , Cu $\mathrm{K}_{\mathfrak{a l}}$ , $2\ \lambda=1.5418\ \mathrm{\AA};$ from 5 ${\bf\Xi}^{\circ})$ to $30~(^{\circ})$ with a step size of 0.02 (°) at a scan speed of $10~(^{\circ})/\mathrm{{min}}$ , and it was reduced to $2(^{\circ})/\mathrm{min}$ for temperature-dependent PXRD measurements.
Scale-up synthesis of JNU-2.
A mixture of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.264\mathrm{~g},\ 1.08\mathrm{~mmol})$ , $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{1.069~g,~3.6~mmol)}$ , adenine $(0.162\ \mathrm{g},\ 1.2\$ mmol), 4-pyrazolecarboxylic acid $(0.264~\mathrm{g},2.35~\mathrm{mmol})$ ), N,NDimethylformamide (DMF, $6~\mathrm{mL}$ ), N,N-Dimethylacetamide (DMA, $6~\mathrm{mL}$ ) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $2~\mathrm{mL}$ ) were placed in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ glass vial and heated at $120{^\circ\mathrm{C}}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ , After it was cooled to room temperature at a rate of $5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , the pale yellow block crystals (as-synthesized JNU-2) were collected and washed with DMF and methanol three times each, then air-dried and heated under high vacuum at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ to afford desolvated JNU-2 $\sim$ $700\quad\mathrm{mg,}\quad\sim\quad40\%$ yield). Anal. calcd. (found) for $(\mathrm{C_{13}H_{8}C u N_{9}O_{4}Z n_{2}}){\cdot14\mathrm{H}_{2}O}$ : C, 19.50 (19.41); H, 4.49 (3.95); N, 15.74 (15.62).
Single Crystal X-ray Diffraction Structure Analysis.
Single crystal diffraction data of as-synthesized and desolvated JNU-2 were collected at room temperature and at $100~\mathrm{K}$ , respectively, via an Oxford Cryo stream system on a XtaLAB PRO MM007-DW diffractometer system equipped with a RA-Micro7HF-MR-DW $\mathrm{\langleCu/Mo}\rangle$ ) $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray generator and HyPix-6000HE Hybrid Photon Counting (HPC) X-ray detector (Rigaku, Japan, Cu $\textstyle\mathrm{K}_{a}.$ , $2\ \lambda\ =\ 1.54178\ \mathrm{~\AA~}$ . The structures were solved and refined using Olex 2 with 'XS' and 'XL' plug-in.
Dynamic Column Breakthrough Experiments.
Column breakthrough experiments were conducted on an in-house lab-scale fixed-bed system. The desolvated JNU-2 sample (ca. $2.4\ \mathrm{g}{}^{\cdot}$ ) was packed into a custom-made stainless steel column $(3.15\mathrm{mm}\mathrm{I.D.}\times450\mathrm{mm})$ with silica wool filling the void space. It was heated in situ at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and high vacuum for $24\mathrm{h}$ before a helium flow was introduced to purge the adsorbent. Then a gas mixture of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ was allowed to flow into the column. Outlet effluent from the column was continuously monitored using gas chromatography (GC7890B, Agilent) with a thermal conductivity detector (TCD). After the breakthrough experiment, the sample was regenerated in situ high vacuum for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature before next measurement.
Solvothermal reaction of two base-metal salts, $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and $Z_{\mathrm{{n}(N O_{3})_{2}}}$ , and two simple organic compounds, 4- pyrazolecarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{PyC)}$ and adenine (Ad), in a mixed $\mathrm{DMF/DMA/H_{2}O}$ solution at $120{^\circ\mathrm{C}}$ for 3 days yielded crystals of as-synthesized JNU-2 (Figure S1), which was solvent exchanged and then high-temperature activated to afford desolvated JNU-2. Single crystal X-ray diffraction revealed that desolvated JNU-2 is indeed a quaternary MOF (two clusters and two linkers) and it crystallizes in cubic space group $F m\overline{{3}}c$ with $\mathbf{a}=\mathbf{b}=\mathbf{c}=43.55\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Table S1). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) of both the as-synthesized and the desolvated JNU-2 exhibited only intense sharp $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ peaks at $933.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ without satellite peaks characteristic to $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ , implying that the $\mathrm{Cu}$ ions are monovalent (Figure S2). Two ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ ions are bridged by two Ad ligands at N-3 and N-9 to form a planar $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Ad})_{2}$ unit, and two of them are crisscrossed into a dimeric $\mathrm{Cu_{4}(A d)_{4}}$ cluster; while in the second cluster, two $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions are bridged by two PyC ligands at the two pyrazole nitrogen atoms, both of which further complete tetrahedral coordination by individually bonding to an Ad ligand of $\mathrm{Cu_{4}(A d)_{4}}$ cluster at N-7 and to a PyC ligand of neighboring dizinc cluster at one of its carboxylate oxygen (Figures S3 and S4). Thus, the network can be broken down to $\mathrm{Cu_{4}(A d)_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{NN-PyC})_{2}(\eta^{1}\mathrm{-COO})_{2}(\eta^{1}\mathrm{-Ad})_{2}$ clusters as secondary building units (SBUs) linked by PyC and Ad ligands (Figures 1a and S4). The $\mathrm{Cu_{4}(A d)_{4}}$ SBU is composed of two symmetry-dependent $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Ad})_{2}$ units, in which the two central $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ rings are in close contact. The distance from centroid to centroid of the two $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ rings is about $3.45\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , indicating strong cuprophilic interactions within the dimeric $\mathrm{Cu_{4}(A d)_{4}}$ SBU (Figure S5a). Indeed, the interdimer interaction energy $(\Delta E)$ of the two $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Ad})_{2}$ units was calculated to be –
$92.80\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ (Figure S5b). From the topological point of view, $\mathrm{Cu_{4}(A d)_{4}}$ acts as a 4-connected node and $Z{\mathrm{n}}_{2}(\mathrm{NN}-}$ $\mathrm{PyC)}_{2}(\eta^{1}\mathrm{-COO})_{2}(\eta^{1}\mathrm{-Ad})_{2}$ acts as a 5-connected node, thus, the framework can be simplified as a binodal (4,5)-connected net with a rare xae topology (Figures 1c and S6-S7). Three different cages can be visualized in this framework (Figure 1b). The small cage (cage A, $4.2\mathrm{\AA}$ ) and the large cage (cage C, 9.2 Å) are built with both SBUs as the vertices and both ligands as the edges, while the middle cage (cage B, $7.2\mathrm{\AA})$ is assembled only with the $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{NN-PyC})_{2}(\eta^{\mathrm{1}}\mathrm{-COO})_{2}(\eta^{\mathrm{1}}\mathrm{-Ad})_{2}$ SBU and PyC ligand. In a static view, the framework underlies threedimensional (3D) dumbbell-shaped channels and the point interconnecting cage A and cage $\mathbf{B}$ is of $\sim3.7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in diameter (Figures 1d and S8-S10).
Figure 2. (a) Low-pressure Ar adsorption/desorption isotherms of JNU-2 at $87~\mathrm{K}$ , inset: BET plot for surface area calculation. (b) Calculated pore-size distribution of JNU-2 based on adsorption branch of Ar isotherm at $87\mathrm{~K~}$ . (c) ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ singlecomponent adsorption isotherms of JNU-2 at $298~\mathrm{~K~}$ . (d) Calculated $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ adsorption enthalpy $\left(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}\right)$ of JNU-2.
The phase purity of bulk JNU-2 was determined by comparison between the simulated and experimental powder x-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns (Figure S11). Thermogravimetry and in situ variable-temperature PXRD experiments demonstrated that JNU-2 decomposes at above $400~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ under nitrogen atmosphere (Figure S12). The Energydispersive $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping in conjunction with scanning electron microscopy (SEM) indicated the uniform distribution of $Z\mathrm{n}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}$ elements (Figure S13). To investigate the porosity of JNU-2, the as-synthesized JNU-2 crystals were washed with DMF and methanol, followed by activation at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in vacuo. Ar and ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms of desolvated JNU-2 were measured at 87 and $77\mathrm{K}$ , respectively (Figures 2a, S14 and S15), and both of them showed a fully reversible type-I behavior. A BrunauerEmmett-Teller (BET) surface area of $1219~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a pore volume of $0.56\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ were calculated by using the ASAP 2020 physisorption analyzer's built-in software. The total pore volume of JNU-2 is nearly identical to the theoretical value of $0.54~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (calculated from the crystal structure). The poresize distribution (PSD) of JNU-2 derived from the adsorption branch of Ar isotherm at $87\mathrm{~K~}$ according to the HorvathKawazoe model (pore geometry: slit) reveals three ultramicrospores with peaks centered at 3.4, 4.6 and $6.7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , corresponding to cage A, cage B, and cage C, respectively (Figures 2b and S7). From crystal structure and electronic surface view, the aperture interconnecting cage A and cage B is rich with electronegative nitrogen and oxygen atoms, suggesting the possibility of binding light hydrocarbons, such as $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , through electrostatic interactions including $\mathrm{C-H\cdotsX}$ hydrogen bonding (Figure S16).
Thus, to explore gas adsorption and separation performance of JNU-2, we first measured the pure-component equilibrium adsorption isotherms for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ , $283\mathrm{~K~}$ and $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ under 1 bar (Figures 2c and S17-19). JNU-2 showed large adsorption capacity for both $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ likely due to its high porosity as well as high-density of electronegative nitrogen and oxygen atoms on the surface. More interestingly, the higher uptake of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ than $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ indicates a $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ -selective behavior in JNU-2. Its $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ -uptake reaches $92.0\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (4.19 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ) at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar, higher than most of the benchmark $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ -selective adsorbents that have been studied for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation, such as $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})$ (dobdc) $3.32\ \mathrm{mmol}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1},$ ), TJT-100 $\left(3.84\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ ), $\mathrm{Cu(Qc)}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(1.85\mmol\g^{-1}}$ ), ZIF-7 $(1.85~\mathrm{mmol~g^{-1}}$ ), and MAF-49 $\left(1.73\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1}}\right)$ ), comparable to MUF-15 $\left(4.69\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1}}\right)$ ) under similar conditions (Table S7). Further, the adsorption of both $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ are completely reversible, as evidenced by the continuous gas sorption measurements of JNU-2 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . The fully reproducible adsorption/desorption cycles indicate no oligomerization of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ blocking the channels and no high temperature required for complete regeneration, possible downsides for MOFs with OMSs or extremely strong adsorption sites (Figures S20-21).
The coverage-dependent adsorption enthalpy $\mathrm{(Q_{st})}$ , a quantitative measurement of the binding affinity, was calculated by fitting adsorption isotherms at different temperatures into the virial equation. As shown in Figure 2d, the $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ value for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}(-29.4\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1})}$ is slightly higher than that for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ $(-26.7~\mathrm{~kJ~}\:\mathrm{~mol^{-1}})$ at near-zero coverage, indicative of relatively stronger binding affinity of JNU-2 toward $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ . At higher coverage, the adsorption enthalpy for ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ is maintained at $-\ 25\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , not much different from that for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ . The significantly small adsorption enthalpy for ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ compared to MAF-49 $(-60\mathrm{kJ/mol})^{25}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})$ (dobdc) $\left(-\ 67\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\right)$ confirms no particularly strong adsorption sites in JNU-2 for ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ , which also justifies the complete regeneration at room temperature. Together with the “inversed” $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ adsorption selectivity and high ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ uptake capacity, JNU-2 shows potential as a $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ -selective adsorbent for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation.
Prompted by the suitable size and electrostatic potential of the channel-limiting aperture, we decided to take a closer look at its possible contribution in the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity. Timedependent kinetic adsorption isotherms were measured for ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and up to 0.5 bar (Figure 3a). A notable difference between $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ adsorption kinetics is observed and JNU-2 shows a faster uptake of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ . It is worth pointing out that, instead of reaching maximum adsorption in a short period of time as in open-channel MOFs,37 the adsorption of both ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ in JNU-2 are slow but consistently increasing over time, indicating that these two gas molecules are not freely diffused into the JNU-2 channels, which is in good agreement with our channellimiting assumption that the apertures might control the gas adsorption rate by taking in gas molecule one by one due to its comparable size to ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ molecules.
To further understand the diffusion process in the dumbbellshaped channels of JNU-2, in work reported here we have performed rigid interaction energy scanning along z-axis by using density functional theory (DFT) (Figure 3b and S30), and calculated the interaction potential energy of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ or $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ adsorbed on JNU-2 as a function of the distance from mass center of gas molecule to aperture center. As we expected, the interaction potential energy is dependent on the relative position of gas molecule to the aperture pocket, and the energy reaches maximum $(-~32.3~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6},-26.1\ k J\ m o l^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ ) when passing through the aperture (Figures 3c-d and
S28). Such an energy difference $\mathrm{(>6~kJ~mol^{-1}}$ ) at passing through the aperture seems large enough to generate appreciable selectivity of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ over $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ .
To make a quantitative comparison of these host-guest interactions in JNU-2, we performed detailed structural analysis and energy decomposition analysis of the interaction energy between gas molecules and channel model. In the optimized host-guest structures, the strongest interaction sites for both $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ are very close to the narrow apertures interconnecting cage A and cage B (Figures $4\mathrm{a}$ , S8 and S28- 29), and the calculated interaction energies $\Delta E$ , $\Delta E{=}E_{\mathrm{MOF+gas}}$ $-E_{\mathrm{MOF}}-E_{\mathrm{gas}})$ at these sites are – 32.3 and $-26.1\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , respectively (Table S5), which are consistent with the experimental $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ values $(-29.4\$ and $-26.7\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ at near-zero coverage). As shown in Figure 4b, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ forms four weak hydrogen bonds with JNU-2. In contrast, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ only forms two weak hydrogen bonds. The difference in electrostatic interaction $(7.7\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , mainly hydrogen bonding) seems appropriate to reflect the overall host-guest interaction difference $(6.2\mathrm{\kJ/mol})$ (Table S5). Although the interaction energy difference between $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ doesn’t seem as significant as MAF-49 or $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})$ (dobdc), we suspect a large number of such apertures along the dumbbell-shaped channels could function as multi-stage screening sites as gas molecules diffusing into the framework and gradually amplify the selectivity.
The industrial production of polymer-grade $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ by steam cracking usually contains 6 to $10\%$ of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ as major byproduct and other impurities such as $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .38 To evaluate the practical separation capacity of JNU-2, dynamic column breakthrough experiments were performed under dry condition, in which $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture of 10/90 and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{2}}$ mixture of 10/87/3 were respectively introduced over a packed column of the activated JNU-2 sample $(2.4\ \mathrm{g})$ at rate of 2.0 mL $\mathrm{{min^{-1}}}$ (Figures 4d and S33). Breakthrough curves reveal that JNU-2 can efficiently realize complete separation of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ under ambient conditions. As shown in Figure 4d and e, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ first breaks through the column to yield an outflow of pure $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (over $99.99\%$ ) with no detectable ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ or $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (below the detection limit of thermal conductivity detector), whereas ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ is more preferentially adsorbed in JNU-2 and breaks through the column after a substantial time lapse (Figure S34). For practical industrial applications, the ideal separating materials should also have good recyclability. Thus, we implemented three dynamic breakthrough experiments for a $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture under the similar operating conditions after regenerated in vacuum at room temperature, and the results showed that there was no noticeable loss in the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ separation capacity of JNU-2 (Figure 4d).
The excellent breakthrough results encouraged us to further contrast JNU-2’s separation performance with other ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}.$ - selective adsorbents. ZIF-8 is selected for its comparable adsorption selectivity and MUF-15 for its comparable adsorption capacity (Table S7). Breakthrough experiments were performed for a $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ (50/50) mixture over a packed bed of ZIF-8, MUF-15, or JNU-2 at an inlet flow rate of $1.0~\mathrm{\mL~\min^{-1}}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure 5a). $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ purity and productivity were estimated from their individually breakthrough curves (Figure 5a). As we can see, about 21.2 liters of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ with over $99.99\%$ purity could be retrieved from a $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ (50/50) mixture for $1~\mathrm{kg}$ of JNU-2 (Figure S35 and Table S6), which is higher than most of the benchmark ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -selective adsorbents, such as MUF-15 $(6.6~\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{kg})$ and MAF-49 $(6.2~\mathrm{L/kg})$ , comparable to the top-performing TJT100 $(16~\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{kg})$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})$ (dobdc) $(19.93~\mathrm{L/kg})$ (Table S7). Moreover, JNU-2 can easily recover its separation capacity after room-temperature regeneration as evidenced by the breakthrough cycling test (Figure 4e).
Water vapor could have negative impacts on the uptake capacity and long-term stability of many physisorbents.39-41 Large and consistent separation capacity upon exposure to water or water vapor is a prerequisite for porous materials to be viable in gas purification since moisture is inevitable in real industrial processes. JNU-2 exhibits excellent stability in water for at least 30 days, in pH 2 or 12 aqueous solutions for over 15 days at room temperature, which are confirmed by the retained peaks and relative intensities in their corresponding PXRD patterns (Figure 6a) as well as the retained ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms (Figure 6b). JNU-2, therefore, is an ideal platform for studying the influence of humid on gas separation by pre-excluding the possibility of framework decomposition. As shown in Figure 6c, the amount of water adsorbed in JNU2 is very low prior to $20\%$ RH (relative humidity), a water vapor uptake of only $0.069~\mathrm{g}$ per gram of sorbent at $20\%$ RH due to the lack of strong hydrophilic interactions. This could be highly convenient for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation under low humid conditions ( $\mathit{\check{\Psi}}_{<}\mathit{\Psi}_{<}\mathit{\Psi}_{}$ $20\%$ RH).To further simulate the practical applications of JNU-2 for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ purification, dynamic column experiments were performed to test the material’s ability to separate $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ from an equimolar $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture under ca. $10\%$ RH conditions. Indeed, as shown in Figure 6d, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ first breaks through the column, and then, ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ breaks through the column after a substantial time lapse. About 10.2 liters of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ with over $99.99\%$ purity could be retrieved from a $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ (50/50) mixture for $1~\mathrm{kg}$ of JNU-2 (Figure S36). More importantly, the breakthrough experiments are fully reproducible upon room-temperature regeneration under vacuum, and three cycles of breakthrough curves are largely overlapped, indicating that JNU-2 could offer excellent separation capacity even under humid conditions and its viability for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ purification under real application conditions.
In conclusion, we synthesized a multicomponent microporous MOF (JNU-2) underlying a rare xae topology, its cage-like cavities are interconnected through apertures with a limiting diameter of $\sim3.7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . The precise line-up of O-atoms on the aperture renders JNU-2 with an enhanced ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ selectivity over $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ by forming a couple of more $\mathrm{C-H\cdotsO}$ hydrogen bonds with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ . Furthermore, the excellent water and $\mathfrak{p H}$ stability of JNU-2 promise its potential applications under real conditions. Dynamic breakthrough experiments under humid conditions confirm JNU-2’s exceptional capacity of separating $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ from $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and its performance is not dramatically affected by the presence of water vapor due to its insignificant water uptake at low humidity. By illustrating a MOF material with large cavities interconnected with tailored apertures, this work successfully demonstrated a strategy for both high ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ adsorption capacity and high ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ over $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ selectivity through a multi-stage screening mechanism.
